(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 41-year-old describes severe stabbing pain under one fingernail, worse in cold water, with an exquisitely tender point. The nail itself looks almost normal, with a small red-blue spot beneath it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Glomus tumour","Nail unit melanoma","Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma","Subungual haematoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the triad of paroxysmal pain, point tenderness and cold sensitivity. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A patient has a single nail with longitudinal erythronychia, distal subungual hyperkeratosis and splinter haemorrhages. Which benign tumours does the deck associate with this pattern?","choices":["Glomus tumour or pyogenic granuloma","Digital myxoid cyst or subungual exostosis","Melanocytic naevus or lentigo","Onychopapilloma or onychomatricoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — single-nail longitudinal change with distal hyperkeratosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Four patients each have a pigmented band in a nail. Which pattern of longitudinal melanonychia should worry you most?","choices":["A band that grows out distally over several months after a crush injury","A band present in all twenty nails since adolescence, identical in every digit","A new or evolving band in ONE digit, increasing in width","Uniform narrow bands in SEVERAL nails, present since childhood and unchanged in width or colour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — single-digit change with widening is the concerning pattern. The full description also includes irregular colour, thickness or spacing of the lines, and proximal widening or a triangular shape. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 71-year-old retired roofer has several rough patches on his bald scalp. Each is about 4 mm, pink, and you notice them more when you run a gloved finger over the scalp than when you look. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Superficial basal cell carcinoma","Solar lentigo","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the sandpaper texture is often felt before it is seen. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has a brown band running the length of the left thumbnail. Over eight months it has widened, the lines within it vary in colour and spacing, and pigment now extends onto the skin of the proximal nail fold. Which finding is most concerning, and what is it called?","choices":["The pigment extending onto the proximal nail fold — the Hutchinson sign, highly concerning for nail unit melanoma","The widening of the band — the Hutchinson sign, highly concerning for nail unit melanoma","The variation in line colour and spacing — the Hutchinson sign, concerning for a benign melanonychia","The eight-month duration — the Hutchinson sign, concerning for a subungual haematoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it should prompt urgent expert evaluation regardless of other features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man of Mediterranean origin has slowly progressive purple plaques on both lower legs over several years. He is not immunosuppressed. Which form of Kaposi sarcoma is this?","choices":["Iatrogenic — occurring with immunosuppressive therapy and improving when doses are reduced","Epidemic — occurring in acquired immunodeficiency and treated first with antiretroviral therapy","Classic — older men, chronic course, rarely fatal, related to age-associated immune senescence","Endemic — often aggressive in young Black men in equatorial Africa and sometimes rapidly fatal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the chronic indolent form. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 60-year-old is being examined for a suspicious lesion. Which additional sites does the deck ask you to include, and when is the oral cavity specifically indicated?","choices":["Palms, soles and nails in every patient; the oral mucosa specifically when melanoma is possible; and the regional nodes for every lesion whatever its risk","The affected area alone, since the total-body survey, the acral sites and the regional nodes all belong to the dermatologist","The scalp and trunk only, since acral sites and the oral mucosa are rarely involved in cutaneous malignancy","Palms, soles and nails in all; the oral mucosa specifically when Kaposi sarcoma is possible; regional nodes for invasive or high-risk disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the oral examination is tied to Kaposi sarcoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A kidney transplant recipient has had two squamous cell carcinomas removed this year. He asks whether anything can lower his chances of getting more. What do you tell him?","choices":["Reducing his immunosuppression is the only measure that lowers his risk","Nicotinamide five hundred milligrams twice daily reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about thirty per cent in high-risk patients","Niacin five hundred milligrams twice daily reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about thirty per cent","Nicotinamide five hundred milligrams twice daily reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about twenty per cent overall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and it is specifically described as a strategy for transplant recipients. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 63-year-old's melanoma is reported at 1.8 mm Breslow thickness. Which re-excision margin applies, and what else does that thickness trigger?","choices":["A 1 cm margin, and referral to an expert centre because the lesion is deeper than 1 mm","A 0.5 to 1 cm margin, and routine dermatology follow-up without expert-centre referral","A 1 to 2 cm margin, with referral reserved until nodal disease is demonstrated","A 1 to 2 cm margin, and referral to an expert centre because the lesion is deeper than 1 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same threshold governs both. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 39-year-old has a 4 mm pigmented lesion that is asymmetric with an irregular border and has darkened over three months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer for sentinel lymph node biopsy before sampling the lesion","Biopsy or excise it, because smaller lesions can still be melanoma","Reassure and review in a year, because a lesion under 6 mm cannot be melanoma","Photograph and review in six months, because evolution over three months is too rapid for melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the 6 mm figure is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 58-year-old has just had her first basal cell carcinoma excised. She asks how often she needs to be seen from now on. What is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["At least an annual full-skin examination, because a second basal cell carcinoma develops in up to half of patients","Review only if she notices a new or changing lesion herself","A full-skin examination every three months for life","No further skin surveillance is needed, since the lesion was completely excised with clear margins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the up-to-fifty-per-cent second-primary rate is what makes annual review mandatory. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a pigmented lesion excised, reported as melanoma with a positive sentinel node. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the sentinel node biopsy to confirm the finding before any referral to an expert centre is made","Begin topical imiquimod to the excision site while awaiting further staging and imaging","Refer to an expert centre, since nodal spread is an explicit referral trigger and coordinated dermatology and surgical oncology care is warranted","Arrange routine dermatology follow-up, since a positive sentinel node does not alter the management pathway or the referral threshold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — nodal or other-site spread crosses the threshold regardless of thickness. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient with an advanced basal cell carcinoma is no longer a surgical candidate. Which systemic option does the deck give?","choices":["A hedgehog pathway inhibitor","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel","Romidepsin or vorinostat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reserved for advanced or metastatic disease or extensive tumour burden. Vismodegib or sonidegib. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 30-year-old Black patient asks whether she needs to worry about melanoma. Which is the most accurate response using the deck's figures?","choices":["Lifetime risk is lower but not zero — about 0.1 to 0.5% in persons of colour against about 2% in white individuals","Lifetime risk is effectively zero in persons of colour, so routine skin checks are unnecessary","Lifetime risk is the same in all skin tones, at about 2%","Lifetime risk is higher in persons of colour, at about 2% against 0.1 to 0.5% in white individuals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lower risk, and acral and nail sites still need checking. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old transplant recipient has a firm nodule on his lower lip. You are arranging a biopsy. What must the sample allow the pathologist to determine?","choices":["The Breslow thickness in millimetres, to assign a T category","The histopathologic subtype alone, since that dictates treatment selection","Whether human herpesvirus 8 is present in the specimen","Whether the disease is in situ or invasive, along with the histologic risk features"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sufficient depth is what makes that distinction possible, and it changes everything downstream. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"One of that 71-year-old's scalp lesions is now thickened, indurated and has bled twice. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin field-directed fluorouracil to the whole scalp and review at the end of the course","Reassure and review in a year, since actinic keratoses commonly involute without treatment","Order a shave or punch biopsy, because induration and bleeding raise concern for squamous cell carcinoma","Treat with liquid nitrogen cryotherapy and review in three months, since these are expected features"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those features are not part of a typical actinic keratosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 74-year-old farmer has eleven actinic keratoses across his forehead and temples. Which approach does the deck favour, and why?","choices":["Immediate wide excision of the whole affected field, because eleven lesions cannot be treated topically","Field-directed therapy, because multiple lesions in one anatomic region reflect field cancerization risk","Lesion-directed cryotherapy to each of the eleven in turn, because each lesion carries its own independent risk","Observation with annual review, because eleven lesions in one region indicates indolent disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — options are fluorouracil, imiquimod, photodynamic therapy, or fluorouracil plus calcipotriene. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient with early patch-stage cutaneous T-cell lymphoma is to begin treatment. Which initial skin-directed options does the deck list?","choices":["Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil, photodynamic therapy and cryotherapy","Liposomal doxorubicin, paclitaxel and radiation","Topical corticosteroids, topical mechlorethamine, bexarotene gel","Methotrexate, extracorporeal photopheresis, systemic bexarotene and romidepsin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — four skin-directed options for early disease. Ultraviolet phototherapy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A melanoma is excised and reported at 1.3 mm Breslow thickness with no ulceration. What does the deck say about sentinel lymph node biopsy here, and what should the patient be told it achieves?","choices":["It should be offered or discussed, since the threshold is 1.0 mm; it is a therapeutic procedure that reliably improves overall survival","It should not be offered, since the threshold is 4.0 mm and this lesion is far below it","It should not be offered, since sentinel node biopsy is reserved for patients with palpable nodes","It should be offered or discussed, since the threshold is 1.0 mm; it is a staging procedure and may not itself improve overall survival"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an honest expectation to set. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A patient has just had a basal cell carcinoma excised and asks whether that is the end of it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["A second basal cell carcinoma develops in up to 50% of patients, so at least annual full-skin examination is mandatory","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in under 1% of patients, so no further surveillance is needed","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in up to 50% of patients, so examination every five years is sufficient","Recurrence at the same site is near-certain, so a further excision should be booked now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the second-primary risk is what drives lifelong surveillance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 62-year-old has a biopsy-confirmed superficial basal cell carcinoma on his upper back, with no high-risk features. He would prefer to avoid surgery. Which option is appropriate, and what must follow it?","choices":["Fluorouracil twice daily for two weeks, with clinical clearance confirmed afterwards","Vismodegib, since it avoids surgery altogether","Imiquimod five nights weekly for six to ten weeks, with clinical clearance confirmed afterwards","Imiquimod five nights weekly for six to ten weeks, with no further review needed if the lesion disappears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is acceptable for selected superficial disease, but clearance must be verified. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has a biopsy-confirmed superficial basal cell carcinoma on the upper back, with no high-risk features. Which topical regimen fits the deck, and what must follow it?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy every 2 to 3 weeks for 12 weeks; clearance must be confirmed afterwards","Imiquimod five nights weekly for 6 to 10 weeks, or fluorouracil twice daily for up to 12 weeks; clinical clearance must be confirmed afterwards","Imiquimod twice daily for 6 to 10 weeks, or fluorouracil five nights weekly for up to 12 weeks; clearance can be assumed once inflammation settles","Vismodegib orally for 6 to 10 weeks; clinical clearance must be confirmed afterwards"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — confirming clearance is part of the regimen, not optional. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man newly diagnosed with human immunodeficiency virus has several red-purple plaques on his legs and is not yet on antiretroviral therapy. Which is the first priority in his Kaposi sarcoma management?","choices":["Reduce his immunosuppressive medication doses, since dose reduction is the cornerstone of epidemic disease","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel, since systemic chemotherapy is the cornerstone of epidemic disease","Begin intralesional vinblastine to each plaque, since local palliation is the cornerstone of epidemic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — antiretroviral therapy comes before local or systemic cancer treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A patient with early-stage cutaneous T-cell lymphoma asks whether aggressive chemotherapy now would cure it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Total-skin electron-beam treatment should be given first to every patient regardless of stage","Early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure the disease or prevent progression, and may itself cause harm","Early aggressive systemic chemotherapy reliably cures the disease and prevents progression, so it should begin immediately","No treatment of any kind alters the course, so observation alone is appropriate at every stage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — overly aggressive therapy may cause complications and premature death, so a stage-directed, skin-first approach suits most early disease. Aggression is a harm here rather than a virtue. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 61-year-old kidney transplant recipient develops Kaposi sarcoma. Which is the most appropriate management step, and what caution applies?","choices":["Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment","Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel first, since iatrogenic disease does not respond to dose reduction","Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses where clinically feasible, coordinating with the transplant team before making any change","Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses immediately without involving the transplant team, since the malignancy takes priority"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the transplant team must be involved before doses change. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has a chronic pruritic eruption that has not settled on treatment. Which two clinical clues should raise suspicion of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Annular plaques with central clearing, and a positive potassium hydroxide preparation from the border","Itch out of proportion to the apparent inflammatory activity, and follicular involvement with hair loss","Complete absence of itch, and sparing of the hair follicles throughout the eruption","Rapid complete response to a topical corticosteroid, and sharply demarcated silvery scale"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — folliculotropism discriminates from routine eczema or psoriasis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"An 82-year-old with classic Kaposi sarcoma has a few troublesome plaques on one shin and no visceral disease. Which local options does the deck give?","choices":["Total-skin electron-beam treatment as the initial approach","Palliative local therapy — intralesional vincristine, vinblastine or bleomycin, or radiation","Antiretroviral therapy first, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment","Hedgehog pathway inhibitors, vismodegib or sonidegib"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy and intralesional vinblastine are also listed for all forms. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a biopsy-proven invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the ear, 1.2 cm across, with perineural invasion on pathology. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer for Mohs micrographic surgery, since the site, size and perineural invasion all meet the criteria","Excise it with a four-millimetre margin in the clinic today and review the pathology report","Begin imiquimod five nights weekly for six to ten weeks","Start programmed death 1 blockade before any surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — high-risk site, over one centimetre on the face, and aggressive histology each independently qualify. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old kidney transplant recipient, six years post-transplant, has developed his fourth squamous cell carcinoma this year. Which does the deck say about this pattern, and what can be offered to reduce new tumours?","choices":["Multiple tumours typically emerge within six weeks of transplant and are usually indolent; nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily reduces new tumours by about 30%","Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant; oral acitretin reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about 30%","Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant; nicotinamide reduces new tumours by about 20%","Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant and are often aggressive; nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about 30%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both the timing and the chemoprevention figure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 68-year-old has a single well-demarcated actinic keratosis on the dorsal hand. Which is the most appropriate treatment, and what should you tell her to expect?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy; the lesion will crust and disappear over 10 to 14 days","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy; the lesion will blister and resolve completely within 24 hours","Topical fluorouracil to the whole forearm; the field will inflame and settle over 10 to 14 days","Surgical excision with 5 mm margins; the wound will heal over 10 to 14 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lesion-directed therapy suits an isolated lesion with clear borders. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"}]};
